From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 09:55:56 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] airconv: double-precision instructions, done in single
 precision

Apple GPUs have no fp64, so every sm5 double instruction (dadd, dmul, dmin,
dmax, deq, dge, dlt, dne, dmov, dmovc, dtof, ftod) and the sm5.1 extended
ones (ddiv, dfma, drcp, dtoi, dtou, itod, utod) hit the converter's
"unhandled dxbc instruction" assert and the game ended with 0x80000101.
The registers keep IEEE double bits in their .xy/.zw pairs; each operation
decodes its doubles to the nearest float (denormals flush to zero, NaN and
infinity kept), computes in single precision and encodes the result back.
dmov and dmovc copy the bits exactly. 64-bit immediates are read as their
four raw dwords.

Class: feature
Evidence: Resident Evil 2 "R.P.D. Demo" (app-1168280) on DXMT ended at its first compute shader with "Assertion failed: (0 && \"unhandled dxbc instruction\")" (report 20261124-094946); on Vulkan DXVK refused the same shader (createComputeShader E_INVALIDARG): KosmicKrisp has no shaderFloat64.
Offered-upstream: no
